1
00:00:01,140 --> 00:00:02,380
It's so hot...

2
00:00:04,760 --> 00:00:06,800
Good morning.

3
00:00:07,240 --> 00:00:09,220
Thank you for riding with us.

4
00:00:10,720 --> 00:00:13,540
I'm supposed to be in
the System Division, but...

5
00:00:14,140 --> 00:00:16,520
Lately all I do is help out in the field.

6
00:00:21,180 --> 00:00:22,206
Could it be...

7
00:00:23,140 --> 00:00:25,640
There's some other motive behind this...

8
00:00:26,220 --> 00:00:27,840
N-no way...

9
00:00:28,140 --> 00:00:29,600
It's just training, right?

10
00:00:29,940 --> 00:00:32,320
To learn how operations work...

11
00:00:35,740 --> 00:00:39,400
♫ July: station staff at Fukuzumi

12
00:00:39,760 --> 00:00:43,660
♫ Second half: mad train modifications

13
00:00:43,840 --> 00:00:47,600
♫ August: conductor training course

14
00:00:47,820 --> 00:00:51,380
♫ The Kraepelin test was brutal

15
00:00:51,840 --> 00:00:55,340
♫ September: conductor practicum

16
00:00:55,400 --> 00:00:58,860
♫ First day and it's a just-go-for-it schedule

17
00:00:59,004 --> 00:01:02,226
This train will go as far as it can.
♫ Tyurya tyurya tyurya...

18
00:01:02,860 --> 00:01:08,256
Please be aware that the train type,
arrival times, and connections may change.

19
00:01:08,300 --> 00:01:10,250
♫ October: conductor

20
00:01:10,400 --> 00:01:11,470
♫ Tyurya rya

21
00:01:11,740 --> 00:01:18,740
Yeah... this is definitely a demotion, isn't it?

22
00:02:31,360 --> 00:02:32,453
Good morn--

23
00:02:32,590 --> 00:02:33,620
Huh...?

24
00:02:34,110 --> 00:02:37,040
The System Division, after so long, was completely empty.

25
00:02:38,040 --> 00:02:39,790
The attendance log... let's see.

26
00:02:39,960 --> 00:02:42,060
Lt. Commander is on leave at Risa.

27
00:02:43,180 --> 00:02:44,720
Hey--

28
00:02:51,520 --> 00:02:53,690
Ahh, an endless-summer resort... nice...

29
00:02:54,170 --> 00:02:55,193
Huh?

30
00:02:55,550 --> 00:02:57,630
Tarumi-senpai's name isn't here...

31
00:02:58,400 --> 00:03:00,120
"Akane, did you hear?"

32
00:03:00,600 --> 00:03:04,410
"They say there's a ghost train."

33
00:03:05,160 --> 00:03:08,880
Don't tell me she ran off chasing that ghost train rumor...

34
00:03:10,810 --> 00:03:12,080
Morning...

35
00:03:12,480 --> 00:03:14,920
Oh, Section Chief.
Good morning.

36
00:03:16,320 --> 00:03:17,406
Um...

37
00:03:17,560 --> 00:03:19,000
Sorry about the short notice, but...

38
00:03:23,790 --> 00:03:25,980
And so, I was seconded to a subsidiary.

39
00:03:26,650 --> 00:03:30,840
Well, I did get the title of "Senior Staff,"
so the cushy transfer isn't all bad.

40
00:03:32,200 --> 00:03:34,780
This room all the way in the back is Development Section 1.

41
00:03:35,200 --> 00:03:36,270
Hey there.

42
00:03:38,653 --> 00:03:39,660
Good morning.

43
00:03:39,706 --> 00:03:40,773
Good morning.

44
00:03:43,780 --> 00:03:44,880
'Sup.

45
00:03:44,905 --> 00:03:46,026
Yo.

46
00:03:48,600 --> 00:03:49,640
Hey.

47
00:03:49,693 --> 00:03:50,766
'Ey.

48
00:03:56,600 --> 00:03:57,660
Ah...

49
00:03:57,719 --> 00:03:58,832
...hey.

50
00:03:58,890 --> 00:04:00,090
...whoa!?

51
00:04:01,063 --> 00:04:02,136
Hmm?

52
00:04:02,166 --> 00:04:04,313
(Yuina) Pshhh! Clackety-clack!

53
00:04:04,750 --> 00:04:05,750
(Akane) Oh!
(Yuina) Oh!

54
00:04:07,320 --> 00:04:09,020
So you got seconded too, Senpai?

55
00:04:10,140 --> 00:04:12,440
Cardan drive is nice, but you
really can't beat nose-suspended!

56
00:04:13,040 --> 00:04:14,040
Huh?

57
00:04:14,560 --> 00:04:17,580
What!?
You love the sound of a DMH-17 engine!?

58
00:04:17,920 --> 00:04:20,030
You get it! You really get it!

59
00:04:20,616 --> 00:04:24,872
(Akane) This person has gone completely off the rails.
(Yuina) I actually have a KiHa 20 in preservation on my home siding!

60
00:04:24,993 --> 00:04:27,160
(Yuina) The sound is just amazing!

61
00:04:27,430 --> 00:04:28,879
No...

62
00:04:29,020 --> 00:04:32,430
It seems she's not the only one
who's derailed around here.

63
00:04:40,620 --> 00:04:42,350
How did it come to this...

64
00:04:42,970 --> 00:04:46,670
Senpai, this isn't a train yard.
It's a subsidiary.

65
00:04:50,720 --> 00:04:52,560
Give me a straight answer!

66
00:04:52,620 --> 00:04:53,726
Huh?

67
00:04:53,870 --> 00:04:56,510
Can you really have this
running by end of next month?

68
00:04:57,390 --> 00:04:58,510
We'll do our best.

69
00:04:58,633 --> 00:04:59,773
(Katsuragi) That goes without sayin'!
(Yuina) Deputy Manager Yumesaki--

70
00:04:59,820 --> 00:05:01,713
(Yuina) --is getting chewed out again...
(Katsuragi) Can you do it or can't you!

71
00:05:01,982 --> 00:05:04,969
(Akane) The one yelling is our General Affairs director, right?
(Yumesaki) We'll do our best. (Katsuragi) That's a given!

72
00:05:05,248 --> 00:05:07,762
(Yuina) Yeah, he's the client for this project.
(Yumesaki) By the go-live date...

73
00:05:07,882 --> 00:05:08,915
(Akane) Ah...
(Katsuragi) Well!?

74
00:05:08,982 --> 00:05:10,057
(Yuina) Even so...

75
00:05:10,082 --> 00:05:13,029
(Yuina) That attitude toward a subsidiary, though?
(Yumesaki) By the go-live date... (Katsuragi) So it'll be done, yeah?

76
00:05:16,880 --> 00:05:18,670
Like I'd trust that!

77
00:05:18,740 --> 00:05:19,740
I'm terribly sorry...

78
00:05:19,790 --> 00:05:22,010
Just 'cause you're a subsidiary
don't mean you can slack off!

79
00:05:22,170 --> 00:05:24,570
The whole company's paying for this!

80
00:05:25,310 --> 00:05:27,530
I'm terribly sorry...

81
00:05:27,960 --> 00:05:30,520
Does being the parent company
really make you that important...?

82
00:05:32,220 --> 00:05:33,440
Oh, Akane?

83
00:05:35,610 --> 00:05:36,610
Um...

84
00:05:37,920 --> 00:05:39,740
A development project this big--

85
00:05:40,140 --> 00:05:42,410
How come it didn't go through
the System Division?

86
00:05:46,876 --> 00:05:47,876
Huh...

87
00:05:47,930 --> 00:05:49,880
Did I say something weird...?

88
00:05:54,730 --> 00:05:56,170
Well, to begin with...

89
00:05:58,570 --> 00:06:02,430
Part of it was the System Division getting
sidelined after the XP incident, but...

90
00:06:02,670 --> 00:06:05,420
Originally, this was supposed to be
an easy project.

91
00:06:06,090 --> 00:06:09,130
The plan was simply to customize
a product called Kinoko Workflow

92
00:06:09,260 --> 00:06:13,020
for the General Affairs department
and roll it out.

93
00:06:13,900 --> 00:06:17,520
But midway through the project,
things took a dramatic turn.

94
00:06:18,220 --> 00:06:20,480
Someone who can't read the room
started pushing a "logical" argument,

95
00:06:20,940 --> 00:06:23,150
insisting it be deployed
to every department.

96
00:06:23,640 --> 00:06:24,830
Great idea!

97
00:06:25,240 --> 00:06:27,260
On top of that, the majority of employees--

98
00:06:27,370 --> 00:06:29,920
demanded the adoption of
Takenoko Groupware instead.

99
00:06:30,490 --> 00:06:32,110
The rest, as you can imagine--

100
00:06:32,220 --> 00:06:33,520
A pointless turf war.

101
00:06:34,410 --> 00:06:36,700
In the end, Team Takenoko won big.

102
00:06:37,130 --> 00:06:38,880
They were hopelessly outnumbered.

103
00:06:39,870 --> 00:06:42,120
All the work done so far?
Completely scrapped.

104
00:06:43,080 --> 00:06:46,780
However, Kinoko Workflow had
some features too good to throw away,

105
00:06:47,130 --> 00:06:48,780
so they forced a merger.

106
00:06:52,220 --> 00:06:54,970
Then related systems got absorbed too.

107
00:07:15,720 --> 00:07:18,200
And on top of everything,
by executive decree--

108
00:07:18,650 --> 00:07:20,200
"Move it to the cloud!"

109
00:07:23,260 --> 00:07:25,600
And so, the project is now a raging dumpster fire.

110
00:07:25,770 --> 00:07:26,770
Sparkle!

111
00:07:26,970 --> 00:07:30,170
Anyway, it goes live next month! Got it!?

112
00:07:32,250 --> 00:07:35,530
Wait... don't tell me the deadline
is still the original one...

113
00:07:36,440 --> 00:07:37,530
Bingo.

114
00:07:37,920 --> 00:07:39,450
Deadlines don't get extended.

115
00:07:39,690 --> 00:07:41,450
You can't defy the parent company.

116
00:07:43,710 --> 00:07:45,840
A system just needs to work, right?

117
00:07:46,410 --> 00:07:49,050
You'll regret that in about eight years.

118
00:07:49,690 --> 00:07:51,710
Not my pro-blem!

119
00:07:53,800 --> 00:07:54,830
Ah...

120
00:07:57,766 --> 00:07:58,781
Oh...

121
00:07:58,806 --> 00:08:02,490
That famous ghost train rumor...
could it be...

122
00:08:15,530 --> 00:08:16,540
The horror here...

123
00:08:17,040 --> 00:08:18,480
...is worse than any ghost.

124
00:08:22,290 --> 00:08:23,290
Well, well.

125
00:08:23,320 --> 00:08:26,060
Found an SQL injection
vulnerability.

126
00:08:27,035 --> 00:08:28,035
This is...

127
00:08:28,060 --> 00:08:30,720
Information that shouldn't be visible
is showing up...

128
00:08:31,230 --> 00:08:34,510
In fact, they probably don't even
understand the business requirements...

129
00:08:35,560 --> 00:08:36,810
S-Q-L In-what-tion?

130
00:08:37,000 --> 00:08:38,810
What's that? Some kind of train?

131
00:08:40,350 --> 00:08:42,970
Shall I send you
to the scrap line?

132
00:08:43,550 --> 00:08:48,520
Not the scrap line! No!
I don't wanna be bulldozer food!

133
00:08:48,970 --> 00:08:50,160
Good morning.

134
00:08:50,860 --> 00:08:52,400
Good MOHAning, SuHaFu!

135
00:08:52,840 --> 00:08:55,200
My junior's transformation has me all ORONErvous!

136
00:08:55,520 --> 00:08:58,410
Hiya! I'm Senior Staff Yuina Tarumi!

137
00:08:58,680 --> 00:09:00,120
(Yuina) Ahahahahaha!

138
00:09:00,120 --> 00:09:01,690
(Akane) *kick*
(Yuina) OW-OW-MAITE-TE-TE...!

139
00:09:02,650 --> 00:09:03,930
So, Senior Staff Tarumi--

140
00:09:04,270 --> 00:09:06,910
Have you ever heard of
SQL injection?

141
00:09:07,470 --> 00:09:08,800
Akane, you're no fun.

142
00:09:09,020 --> 00:09:10,920
Senior Staff Hosono, you're no fun.

143
00:09:11,900 --> 00:09:14,280
SQL In-whatever-tion, huh...

144
00:09:15,180 --> 00:09:17,470
Oh! I think I saw it on
the IT certification exam!

145
00:09:17,980 --> 00:09:18,980
That's it.

146
00:09:19,610 --> 00:09:20,610
I only saw it, okay?

147
00:09:20,750 --> 00:09:23,180
I don't understand SQL at all!

148
00:09:23,760 --> 00:09:26,640
She really does sneak in station names
at every opportunity...

149
00:09:27,230 --> 00:09:28,940
And you call yourself an engineer!?

150
00:09:29,950 --> 00:09:33,240
In upstream processes you don't
need to write code, so it's fine!

151
00:09:33,580 --> 00:09:34,920
No, it is NOT fine!

152
00:09:35,820 --> 00:09:37,710
Daishōgun? Arima Onsen-guchi?

153
00:09:41,500 --> 00:09:43,610
Okay, forget SQL for now--

154
00:09:43,790 --> 00:09:46,960
Let me explain injection attacks
in general terms.

155
00:09:47,370 --> 00:09:51,850
First, let's say this is the world
inside an anime that you like, Senpai.

156
00:09:52,030 --> 00:09:53,050
Let's say it is!

157
00:09:54,600 --> 00:09:56,000
Here we have a script.

158
00:09:56,170 --> 00:09:58,890
Ooh...
It's like nothing of this world.

159
00:09:59,240 --> 00:10:00,890
There's a blank field in it.

160
00:10:01,500 --> 00:10:03,390
You can write any dialogue you want!

161
00:10:03,800 --> 00:10:06,220
Yes. You can write any text you want.

162
00:10:06,760 --> 00:10:08,400
And whatever you write will happen.

163
00:10:08,760 --> 00:10:09,840
Yay!

164
00:10:12,110 --> 00:10:14,330
Akane: "The Montreal monorail!"

165
00:10:15,210 --> 00:10:16,840
What are you making me say?

166
00:10:19,390 --> 00:10:21,720
Is that really the worst you can do with it?

167
00:10:22,235 --> 00:10:23,235
Hmm...

168
00:10:23,260 --> 00:10:26,300
With just dialogue, you can't do much...

169
00:10:27,100 --> 00:10:29,850
Akane: "For example, you can do this."

170
00:10:30,480 --> 00:10:31,980
Hey, that's cheating!
A closing quote!

171
00:10:32,940 --> 00:10:36,910
At that moment, Yuina, seized by an impulse,
suddenly dashes out of the room.

172
00:10:37,230 --> 00:10:39,260
Wha--hey!

173
00:10:41,790 --> 00:10:45,530
When Yuina returned, she was holding
a baked sweet potato.

174
00:10:46,880 --> 00:10:49,850
Yuina: "Did someone order a sweet potato?"

175
00:10:51,640 --> 00:10:54,330
Akane: "Ahh, this potato is so warm and fluffy..."

176
00:10:54,750 --> 00:10:56,600
So, well, that's basically how it works.

177
00:10:57,880 --> 00:11:00,320
This sweet potato
is actually pretty good.

178
00:11:00,890 --> 00:11:02,030
This sweetness...

179
00:11:02,250 --> 00:11:03,250
It's from Tokushima!

180
00:11:03,930 --> 00:11:06,170
She really doesn't miss a trick, does she?

181
00:11:06,480 --> 00:11:08,750
I went all the way to the Seaside Route
to buy them!

182
00:11:08,880 --> 00:11:11,100
Thanks.
Anyway, as I was saying...

183
00:11:11,480 --> 00:11:13,100
Finish eating first...

184
00:11:14,430 --> 00:11:18,270
So basically, if you write a closing quote,
you can write stage directions too.

185
00:11:18,430 --> 00:11:19,430
Yes.

186
00:11:23,440 --> 00:11:26,830
Yuina obtains a
train driver's license!

187
00:11:28,480 --> 00:11:31,790
Yuina acquires
another KiHa 20!

188
00:11:35,450 --> 00:11:37,550
Why not just write "deadline extended"?

189
00:11:37,710 --> 00:11:39,960
Better yet, "project canceled"...

190
00:11:40,140 --> 00:11:42,280
Just do your actual work!

191
00:11:42,590 --> 00:11:44,640
You can rewrite the world however you want,

192
00:11:44,720 --> 00:11:46,750
and find out anything you want to know!

193
00:11:47,480 --> 00:11:50,840
That's basically unauthorized access
to the laws of the universe.

194
00:11:51,900 --> 00:11:54,430
I mean, the fact that it even lets you
enter quotation marks--

195
00:11:54,520 --> 00:11:56,030
It's just stupid design, right?

196
00:11:56,360 --> 00:11:57,450
Exactly.

197
00:11:57,760 --> 00:12:00,190
That is a vulnerability
to injection attacks.

198
00:12:00,940 --> 00:12:02,190
The same thing happens with computers.

199
00:12:02,780 --> 00:12:04,670
If an injection attack gets through--

200
00:12:04,750 --> 00:12:08,360
your data can be stolen
or your system can be exploited.

201
00:12:08,990 --> 00:12:11,160
Like making your senpai run errands for you.

202
00:12:12,910 --> 00:12:16,190
To prevent being exploited,
no matter what text is entered--

203
00:12:16,280 --> 00:12:19,210
you need to make sure it doesn't
cause the system to malfunction.

204
00:12:20,280 --> 00:12:21,850
So with that said, Shimoyama-san,

205
00:12:22,280 --> 00:12:25,050
please rewrite this code
to use DbParameter.

206
00:12:25,310 --> 00:12:27,310
If you would, thanks!

207
00:12:30,270 --> 00:12:31,610
No can do.

208
00:12:32,540 --> 00:12:34,650
No can do...

209
00:12:48,160 --> 00:12:49,230
*sigh*...

210
00:12:55,900 --> 00:12:58,250
Things don't work like they used to, do they?

211
00:13:16,620 --> 00:13:17,680
By the way...

212
00:13:18,890 --> 00:13:21,100
I wonder if those two are doing all right.

213
00:13:24,080 --> 00:13:27,210
Something about this whole situation is off.

214
00:13:28,910 --> 00:13:33,340
First, the Corporate Planning department
pushing through unrealistic scope and schedules.

215
00:13:33,550 --> 00:13:36,840
And the General Affairs director
fixated on the original go-live date...

216
00:13:39,070 --> 00:13:41,210
Even this isn't enough to convince them...

217
00:13:41,920 --> 00:13:43,210
In that case...

218
00:13:46,280 --> 00:13:47,550
How did it go? With the CIO?

219
00:13:48,140 --> 00:13:49,550
I finally found out.

220
00:13:49,930 --> 00:13:53,020
The General Affairs director was told
to keep this project on schedule--

221
00:13:53,240 --> 00:13:55,880
directly by the president himself.

222
00:13:57,960 --> 00:13:59,720
That power-tripping director...

223
00:14:00,190 --> 00:14:05,400
And the president, for his part,
apparently already committed to end-of-next-month.

224
00:14:05,680 --> 00:14:07,520
In a magazine interview, no less.

225
00:14:09,470 --> 00:14:11,400
Always trying to save face...

226
00:14:14,400 --> 00:14:17,050
All that does is make the failure
even more public.

227
00:14:18,160 --> 00:14:20,010
Public...!

228
00:14:26,730 --> 00:14:30,030
Even now, we should transfer
the project to our division...!

229
00:14:30,750 --> 00:14:33,450
At this point,
swapping leadership is too risky.

230
00:14:34,140 --> 00:14:37,550
Let's work on convincing everyone
to go with a phased rollout instead.

231
00:14:41,200 --> 00:14:43,470
Tarumi, Hosono--

232
00:14:44,670 --> 00:14:45,710
Please...

233
00:14:47,050 --> 00:14:48,190
Stay safe...

234
00:14:54,520 --> 00:14:58,300
I can't couple anymore...

235
00:15:02,520 --> 00:15:06,490
The more I investigated,
the more abnormal this project turned out to be.

236
00:15:09,720 --> 00:15:11,660
Less than two months until go-live.

237
00:15:12,040 --> 00:15:14,830
And yet, they haven't even
nailed down the goal.

238
00:15:15,580 --> 00:15:18,110
Bugs? Everyone just looks the other way...

239
00:15:20,160 --> 00:15:24,350
If we just call in Lt. Commander, everything'll work out...

240
00:15:26,540 --> 00:15:27,540
Absolutely not.

241
00:15:27,900 --> 00:15:31,160
That person's code is so messy
it should be in the Guinness records.

242
00:15:35,420 --> 00:15:37,040
Just "working" isn't good enough.

243
00:15:37,920 --> 00:15:40,110
The one who'll suffer is future me.

244
00:15:43,310 --> 00:15:45,370
I have a dream.

245
00:15:47,200 --> 00:15:50,220
Someday, I'll live
an easy, lazy life,

246
00:15:50,680 --> 00:15:53,520
sprawled out on a couch--
the dream of becoming a couch potato!

247
00:15:58,430 --> 00:16:02,200
And for that dream, the first priority
is to nail down the project goals.

248
00:16:04,570 --> 00:16:05,770
Deputy Manager Yumesaki!

249
00:16:08,090 --> 00:16:12,860
Cutting the supplementary report feature
entirely... I'm not on board with that.

250
00:16:13,020 --> 00:16:15,210
Yes, personally I agree with you.

251
00:16:16,490 --> 00:16:18,110
However, Assistant Station Master Tsukita--

252
00:16:18,430 --> 00:16:20,800
Are you aware of the current state
of the project?

253
00:16:21,050 --> 00:16:23,290
Of course, I've heard about it, but...

254
00:16:23,740 --> 00:16:25,130
Were you aware that from
the Kyoki Railway labor union--

255
00:16:25,200 --> 00:16:29,000
three people have already been
hospitalized?

256
00:16:29,280 --> 00:16:30,590
Oh, ah...

257
00:16:30,940 --> 00:16:34,620
For the union, forcing people
to keep going in this situation--

258
00:16:34,765 --> 00:16:35,765
would look bad,

259
00:16:35,790 --> 00:16:36,790
wouldn't it?

260
00:16:36,910 --> 00:16:39,210
Ah... yes...

261
00:16:40,410 --> 00:16:44,640
Actually, there's a way to deliver the
feature you want without building it now,

262
00:16:44,800 --> 00:16:46,330
and without cutting it either.

263
00:16:46,840 --> 00:16:50,240
Would you be willing to help me
strategize on how to make it happen?

264
00:16:52,490 --> 00:16:53,980
Please!

265
00:16:54,430 --> 00:16:55,430
Of course!

266
00:16:55,610 --> 00:16:57,160
(Tsukita) H-Hosono...

267
00:16:57,980 --> 00:17:01,480
(Tsukita) P-please, raise your head.
(Hiroka) Looks like it's going well.

268
00:17:03,710 --> 00:17:06,560
The groundwork went
extremely smoothly.

269
00:17:07,320 --> 00:17:11,920
At every workplace I'd been rotated through,
there was always a key person behind the scenes.

270
00:17:12,240 --> 00:17:13,520
Funny, that.

271
00:17:13,610 --> 00:17:15,870
It's almost as if someone
had planned it all along.

272
00:17:25,340 --> 00:17:26,400
Deputy Manager Yumesaki.

273
00:17:26,780 --> 00:17:29,290
The requirements definition
that was stalled is now complete.

274
00:17:30,060 --> 00:17:31,360
What do you think?

275
00:17:31,960 --> 00:17:33,950
Honestly? It's hopeless.

276
00:17:34,570 --> 00:17:37,660
Given the current state,
it'll take another two or three years.

277
00:17:45,630 --> 00:17:47,310
So here's my proposal.

278
00:17:47,450 --> 00:17:49,120
We split the development into phases--

279
00:17:49,310 --> 00:17:53,260
and for this release, prioritize the core modules
and security-related development.

280
00:17:53,740 --> 00:17:59,390
In Phase 1, we give up on custom forms
and use the standard free-text feature instead...

281
00:18:00,080 --> 00:18:01,080
I see...

282
00:18:02,680 --> 00:18:03,800
It was impossible all along...

283
00:18:07,112 --> 00:18:08,112
(Akane) Deputy Manager!
(Yuina) Yumesaki-san!

284
00:18:08,640 --> 00:18:09,640
(Subordinate) Huh, what?

285
00:18:09,770 --> 00:18:11,680
(Subordinate) The deputy manager! The deputy manager!

286
00:18:11,820 --> 00:18:12,820
(Subordinate) Wait, are you okay!?

287
00:18:14,780 --> 00:18:18,520
And with that, the team's morale
plummeted to rock bottom.

288
00:18:32,160 --> 00:18:34,240
Akane, it's over. I can't do this anymore.

289
00:18:34,400 --> 00:18:36,000
Almost everyone's out sick.

290
00:18:36,280 --> 00:18:38,000
Progress has ground to a halt...

291
00:18:42,240 --> 00:18:44,010
It's completely over...

292
00:18:45,470 --> 00:18:46,480
I know.

293
00:18:46,700 --> 00:18:48,880
There's no choice left
but to cancel this project.

294
00:18:50,090 --> 00:18:53,000
Rather than leave behind a disaster
of a garbage system,

295
00:18:53,400 --> 00:18:54,430
we should end it now, with our own hands--

296
00:18:55,710 --> 00:18:56,710
We can't!

297
00:18:56,736 --> 00:18:59,610
If it fails, we'll be stuck
at this subsidiary forever...

298
00:18:59,880 --> 00:19:01,965
No... we'll be made scapegoats,

299
00:19:02,098 --> 00:19:03,770
and fired by next month.

300
00:19:04,620 --> 00:19:07,610
Forget eight years from now--
I'm worried about next month.

301
00:19:09,690 --> 00:19:10,690
That's right.

302
00:19:11,500 --> 00:19:13,280
I gave in to temptation.

303
00:19:17,900 --> 00:19:18,900
Ah...

304
00:19:19,920 --> 00:19:21,450
"This is Fleet Command."

305
00:19:21,980 --> 00:19:24,300
"Return to base immediately."

306
00:19:24,680 --> 00:19:26,000
Farewell, maintainability.

307
00:19:27,130 --> 00:19:28,130
It's fine.

308
00:19:28,190 --> 00:19:30,110
A system just needs to work.
That's all.

309
00:19:35,050 --> 00:19:37,370
State the nature of the emergency.

310
00:19:38,400 --> 00:19:39,530
Lt. Commander!

311
00:19:41,120 --> 00:19:42,120
Huh...!?

312
00:19:43,150 --> 00:19:44,520
Lt. Commander...!

313
00:19:45,020 --> 00:19:49,150
Kalash, it is time to rise!

314
00:19:51,560 --> 00:19:52,560
So fast!

315
00:19:53,260 --> 00:19:54,270
A-amazing!

316
00:19:54,380 --> 00:19:55,710
Holy crap.

317
00:19:56,510 --> 00:19:57,690
What incredible speed...!

318
00:19:58,400 --> 00:20:00,480
That Guinness record is no joke!

319
00:20:00,640 --> 00:20:02,940
Heh. A Guinness record is merely a decoration!

320
00:20:03,480 --> 00:20:04,940
Whoa... incredible...

321
00:20:05,130 --> 00:20:06,670
It's goto art!

322
00:20:06,840 --> 00:20:09,770
Wha--!? She's writing in machine language!?

323
00:20:10,140 --> 00:20:14,190
Behold-- the Low-Maintainability Code Master!

324
00:20:14,780 --> 00:20:16,190
That's insane.

325
00:20:16,750 --> 00:20:19,550
Oh no...
How are we supposed to test this...

326
00:20:20,350 --> 00:20:21,350
No worries.

327
00:20:22,700 --> 00:20:25,230
I brought along the ultimate
testing weapon!

328
00:20:26,810 --> 00:20:28,270
Where am I?

329
00:20:30,590 --> 00:20:31,660
Akane!?

330
00:20:32,890 --> 00:20:35,530
You look terrible. Are you okay...?

331
00:20:35,710 --> 00:20:37,100
Oh, um, yeah...

332
00:20:37,735 --> 00:20:38,735
That's--!

333
00:20:38,760 --> 00:20:41,040
The one who caused the big incident
in Episode 1--

334
00:20:41,130 --> 00:20:44,160
The one who can make
any program crash--

335
00:20:45,610 --> 00:20:48,060
The Ultimate Bug Trigger!

336
00:20:49,660 --> 00:20:52,480
No time to waste!
Let's do this!

337
00:21:19,340 --> 00:21:20,620
Great work out there!

338
00:21:21,020 --> 00:21:23,040
I hear even the General Affairs director was pleased.

339
00:21:23,485 --> 00:21:24,485
Heh heh.

340
00:21:24,510 --> 00:21:25,800
Ha ha...

341
00:21:59,865 --> 00:22:00,865
Section Chief!

342
00:22:00,890 --> 00:22:04,140
It's been a full year since the OS
went out of support!

343
00:22:04,250 --> 00:22:05,710
We need to update it immediately!

344
00:22:06,695 --> 00:22:07,695
We can't.

345
00:22:07,720 --> 00:22:09,240
That's a no-go.

346
00:22:10,250 --> 00:22:14,060
You read my report
on the security issues, right!?

347
00:22:14,270 --> 00:22:16,780
I did, but if we update the OS--

348
00:22:16,890 --> 00:22:18,780
for some reason, that system stops working.

349
00:22:19,350 --> 00:22:21,230
We're looking into a solution, but...

350
00:22:21,440 --> 00:22:23,790
But we're running out of time to stall!

351
00:22:25,240 --> 00:22:26,240
*sigh*...

352
00:22:26,350 --> 00:22:27,500
If only we had the source code--

353
00:22:27,690 --> 00:22:30,350
or at least the detailed design documents,
it would help...

354
00:22:30,620 --> 00:22:32,560
Wait, there aren't any!?

355
00:22:33,740 --> 00:22:35,440
Well, I had a feeling

356
00:22:35,600 --> 00:22:37,440
this was going to happen
all along...

357
00:22:38,590 --> 00:22:40,220
Let's just hope nothing happens...

358
00:22:43,900 --> 00:22:47,180
Section Chief Hosono!

359
00:23:05,500 --> 00:23:06,686
It's happened.

360
00:24:49,850 --> 00:24:50,850
Ahh...

361
00:24:51,240 --> 00:24:53,930
What have I done
to my future self eight years from now?

362
00:24:54,700 --> 00:24:57,230
Even if that is our future...

363
00:24:57,660 --> 00:24:59,960
The future can always
be changed.

364
00:25:00,200 --> 00:25:01,960
Don't you think so,
Ensign?

365
00:25:02,190 --> 00:25:03,190
Huh?

366
00:25:06,360 --> 00:25:07,520
Yeah.

367
00:25:08,270 --> 00:25:09,270
The future...

368
00:25:09,470 --> 00:25:11,230
...can be changed.
